<DocsLayout title="Merging accounts">
  <h1>Merging accounts</h1>
  <p>
    A person can end up with two accounts in one project. One example is a
    Gmail sign-up (with the address <code>bob-at-gmail.com@accounts.example.com</code>)
    alongside a username account <code>bob</code> (<code>bob@accounts.example.com</code>).
    A merge joins them under one account <strong>without deleting
    anything</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a merge does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Whoever asks chooses the <strong>survivor</strong>; the server never
      picks. The other account is <strong>retired</strong>: it becomes
      <code>USER_STATUS_DEACTIVATED</code>, and <code>merged_into_user_id</code>
      names the survivor. All its sessions are revoked, it never signs in again,
      and it can never be reactivated: <code>ReactivateUser</code> refuses it,
      and SCIM no longer sees it. Its row, and everything stored under its id,
      stays.
    </li>
    <li>
      The survivor takes over whatever lets the person keep signing in the way
      they did. It takes the retired account's <strong>username</strong> if it
      has none, so <code>bob</code> now signs in to the survivor. It takes the
      retired account's <strong>password</strong> if it has none. With
      <code>take_address</code>, it takes the retired account's account address,
      and its own old address moves to the retired account so neither is freed
      for someone else.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <code>user.merged</code> event names the retired account and carries
      <code>merged_into_user_id</code>. Applications move whatever they hold
      under the retired id to the survivor.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who can merge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The person</strong>, with <code>MergeAccounts</code> called from the
      survivor's session. They prove they control the other account with its
      identifier (email or username) and password. These are checked as
      <code>PasswordLogin</code> checks them: the same refusal for an unknown
      identifier and a wrong password, the same lockout, and the same per-IP
      limit. An account with two-step verification on cannot be merged by
      password alone, so turn it off there first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An admin</strong>, with <code>MergeUsers</code>, naming both
      accounts. No password is asked.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What cannot be merged</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An account into itself.</li>
    <li>An account that is not active.</li>
    <li>An anonymous account: upgrade it instead.</li>
    <li>A managed child's account, which stays with its guardian.</li>
    <li>An account that is a guardian: move its children first.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These refusals answer <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>.</p>
</DocsLayout>
